Subject: Object.assign must not use the object-spread fast path

What this does
  Routes Object.assign through assignment semantics rather than sharing the
  shape walk used by object spread.

Why we need it
  The spread walk defines properties, while assignment runs a setter on the target,
  refuses a read-only target in strict mode, and orders the copy as the assignment
  operation does.

What it adds
  Object.assign uses the assignment operation for each source property, so a
  setter runs, a read-only target throws as it should, and property order and
  enumerability are those of the assignment. Object literals with a spread keep the
  shape walk they already had. No opcode is added or retired and BC_VERSION is
  unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -19765,11 +19765,38 @@
         return ret;
     }
 
-    /* No exclusion list means plain `{...src}` / Object.assign, which is the
-       shape worth specialising. `setprop` (Object.assign) would additionally
-       have to honour setters on the target; the fast path only accepts targets
-       that cannot have any, so both callers can share it. */
-    if (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(excluded) != JS_TAG_OBJECT) {
+    /* No exclusion list means plain `{...src}`, which is the shape worth
+       specialising.
+
+       SPREAD ONLY, NEVER Object.assign. The fast path writes with
+       JS_DefinePropertyValue, which is exactly CreateDataPropertyOrThrow --
+       what the spec calls for in a spread (ECMA-262 13.2.5.5), where the target
+       is always a fresh object literal and no conflict is possible.
+
+       Object.assign is specified in terms of Set(to, nextKey, propValue, true)
+       (ECMA-262 20.1.2.1 step 5.c.iii.3), and Set differs from Define in three
+       observable ways that this fast path cannot honour: it invokes a setter on
+       the target, it walks the target's PROTOTYPE CHAIN looking for one, and it
+       preserves an existing property's attributes instead of resetting them to
+       C_W_E. An earlier version of this comment claimed the fast path "only
+       accepts targets that cannot have any" setters -- it does not. The scan
+       below inspects the SOURCE shape for JS_PROP_TMASK; the target is only
+       checked for class_id, is_exotic, fast_array and extensible, all of which
+       a target carrying an accessor or a read-only property passes.
+
+       That gap silently broke two things, caught by test262
+       built-ins/Object/assign/target-set-user-error.js (a throwing setter on
+       the target was replaced rather than called, so nothing threw) and
+       target-set-not-writable.js (a non-writable target property was
+       overwritten instead of raising TypeError).
+
+       Gating on !setprop rather than hardening the scan is deliberate: the
+       shipped React Native bundle has 125 spread sites against 11
+       Object.assign, so this keeps the optimization where essentially all of
+       the traffic is, and returns Object.assign to a path that is correct for
+       prototype setters and attribute preservation too -- not merely for the
+       two cases test262 happens to cover. */
+    if (!setprop && JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(excluded) != JS_TAG_OBJECT) {
         ret = JS_CopyDataPropertiesFast(ctx, target, source);
         if (ret != 0)
             return ret < 0 ? -1 : 0;
